<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/expert/planetary-geology">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an expert lesson, and it assumes the ladder beneath it. From <em>Volcanoes
    and Igneous Processes</em> and <em>Earthquakes and Seismology</em> you carry the
    plate-tectonics machinery — magma, boundaries, seismic probing of interiors — and the
    advanced lessons <em>Geochemical Cycles</em> and <em>Paleoclimate and Ice Ages</em> showed
    what that machinery does to air, water and climate over geologic time. Here we run the
    same machinery on every other world we can reach: the comparison is the method.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use comparative planetology deliberately — treat each world as a controlled experiment on a process you first met on Earth;</li>
    <li>argue why plate tectonics is a regime, not a default, using Venus, Mars and Mercury as the contrast cases;</li>
    <li>read a planet's interior from seismology, gravity and magnetism, and trace what a dead dynamo does to an atmosphere;</li>
    <li>compare volcanism across heat sources and solvents, from Io's tidal furnaces to Enceladus's cryovolcanism;</li>
    <li>date surfaces by counting craters, and state what the impact record implies for Earth.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Planetary geology applies Earth's methods to <strong>every body we can reach</strong> —
    and the comparison is the point: Venus shows the greenhouse effect unconstrained,
    Mars shows atmosphere loss, the icy moons show ocean worlds, and the cratered
    surfaces show histories Earth has erased. Earth is one experiment in a solar
    system full of controls. The syllabus's plate-tectonics topics become, here, a
    comparative checklist: did drift happen there? are they layered? where did their
    volcanism come from? did their boundaries ever move? what did hot spots build
    instead? Each topic below runs the plate-tectonics questions on other worlds.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, the neighbours, the ocean moons, the crater clock" widget="equationStep" config={planetCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> press "Walk me through it" and the panel reveals one step
    at a time — method, rocky neighbours, ocean moons, crater clock. The order is the lesson's
    argument in miniature: first the comparative claim, then three bodies of evidence for it.
    Each step is the summary of a full section below, so it pays to walk the panel first and
    again after reading — the second pass should feel like reading the headings of a chapter
    you now understand.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> planetary geology is not a list of facts about other worlds but a
  checklist — drift, layers, volcanism, boundaries, hot spots — run on every body we can reach.
  The first question on the list is Wegener's: did the plates ever move out there?</p>

  <h2>Wegener on other worlds: the drift question, asked everywhere</h2>
  <p>
    The comparative question behind continental drift (2.1): why does Earth alone (among
    the rocky planets we can survey) run plate tectonics? <strong>Venus</strong> —
    nearly Earth's twin in size and internal heat — shows no plate boundaries, no
    ridge-and-trench pattern: its crust appears to have resurfaced catastrophically
    (the leading model: a global volcanic resurfacing perhaps 300–700 million years
    ago, after which the stagnant lid thickened), heat escaping through a single lid
    rather than moving plates. <strong>Mars</strong> is a one-plate planet — the
    Tharsis bulge, a volcano province the size of a continent, grew in place for
    billions of years with the crust stationary above it. <strong>Mercury</strong>
    shrank as its core cooled, wrinkling into lobate scarps kilometers high — a
    contracting, single-plate world. The expert inference: plate tectonics is not a
    default of rocky planets but a regime that needs conditions — enough water in the
    mantle to weaken rock, the right size and heat flow, a lithosphere that can break
    into mobile lids. Earth has them; its neighbours apparently do not or did not —
    and the why is one of geology's live comparative problems.
  </p>

  <h2>Layers of other worlds: differentiation as the universal process</h2>
  <p>
    Layering (2.2) is planetary physics, not Earth's peculiarity: melt or accrete a
    body big enough, and denser materials sink, lighter ones float — <strong>
    differentiation</strong> — giving every major body a core, mantle and crust
    ( compositions differing by what it formed from and how big it got). The
    evidence is planetary-scale seismology and gravity: Apollo seismometers probed
    the Moon's interior (a small core, a thick rigid lid); InSight's seismometer
    (2018–2022) gave Mars its first interior profile — a liquid core about 1,830 km
    in radius, a crust with two or three layers. Magnetism adds history: Earth's
    dynamo runs in its convecting liquid-iron outer core; Mars's dynamo died about
    4 billion years ago (its ancient southern crust is magnetised, its younger
    north is not), and Venus has none today — with direct consequences for their
    atmospheres (the planetary-geology chain: no dynamo, no magnetic shield, solar
    wind strips the air — Mars's story). Even the icy moons are layered: a rock
    core, a water layer, an ice shell — differentiation all the way down the
    size ladder.
  </p>
  <p>
    Isostasy is the gravity bookkeeping that connects layering to landscape, and it
    works everywhere: floating crust stands higher where it is thicker (the Himalaya
    has a deep root; Mars's Tharsis bulge is a load the crust supports by flexure),
    and unloading raises terrain (Scandinavia still rebounding from its ice sheet).
    The planetary version produced a famous measurement: Mercury's lobate scarps
    imply the crust contracted as the huge iron core cooled and shrank, by roughly
    seven kilometres of radius over billions of years — planetary cooling written
    directly into topography, and readable only because the layering story
    (an iron core dominating a small planet) told geologists what to look for.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the Moon has no layers because it's dead, and
    Mercury is solid through." Both are differentiated, like every large body:
    the Moon has a small (~300 km) iron core and a partial melt layer at its
    core–mantle boundary; Mercury has an iron core filling about 85% of its
    radius — outsized, probably by a giant-impact stripping of its mantle. "Dead"
    means the dynamo and tectonics stopped, not that the layering vanished.
    Differentiation happened once and stays.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> differentiation gives every large body a core, mantle and crust,
  and each body's magnetism dates the dynamo its layering once ran — and records when it died.
  With interiors fixed, the next question is what the surfaces do with their heat: where does
  the melt come from, and what is it made of?</p>

  <h2>Magmatism across the Solar System: from Io's furnaces to cold cryovolcanism</h2>
  <p>
    Volcanism (2.3) runs on the same principle everywhere — melt rises where heat
    and pressure interact — but the energy sources differ and the materials follow.
    <strong>Io</strong> (Jupiter's inner large moon) is the most volcanic body known:
    hundreds of active volcanoes, sulphur plains, plumes 300 km high — heated not by
    internal radioactivity but by <strong>tidal kneading</strong>: its eccentric
    orbit between Jupiter and the other moons flexes its rock, and the friction
    melts it. <strong>Earth's Moon</strong> recorded the other extreme: mare basalts
    flooded the near side 3–4 billion years ago from radioactive heating, then
    stopped — a world that had one volcanic youth. <strong>Mars</strong> hosts the
    Solar System's tallest volcano, Olympus Mons (~22 km high, the size of France) —
    possible precisely because Mars lacks plate tectonics: the plume stayed fixed
    under one spot and stacked lava for billions of years, where Earth's moving
    plates would have built a chain instead (Hawaii, island by island). And on the
    icy moons, volcanism switches solvent: <strong>cryovolcanism</strong> — water
    and ammonia lavas at temperatures where water is the "molten rock" — with
    Enceladus's active plumes (sampled by Cassini: water, salts, organics, hydrogen)
    as the working example. Same geology, different cryostat.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Mars and Venus are geologically dead — everything
    interesting happened billions of years ago." InSight recorded well over a thousand
    marsquakes (2018–2022), some of magnitude 4 and above; Mars shows active dust
    avalanches and recurring slope streaks; and a 2023 re-analysis of Magellan radar
    found a volcanic vent on Venus that changed shape between 1991 images — direct
    evidence of active volcanism this geologic instant. Old surfaces record old
    history; they do not prove a stopped machine, and "dead planet" is a hypothesis
    the data keep partially overturning.
  </p>

  <h2>Boundaries elsewhere: rifting, wrinkling and the absence of subduction</h2>
  <p>
    Without plate boundaries (2.4–2.7), other worlds deform differently, and the
    signatures are legible. Venus shows extensional graben and vast coronae
    (circular uplifts, plume candidates) but no subduction zones — the stagnant-lid
    signature; its "pancake domes" are lava too viscous to spread. Mars shows the
    Valles Marineris — a 4,000-km tectonic rift system, the Solar System's largest
    canyon, opened by the Tharsis load stretching the crust, with no subduction to
    ever close it. Mercury's lobate scarps record global contraction (the crust
    wrinkling as the interior cooled — the opposite of Earth's isostatic quiet).
    The Moon's mascons (mass concentrations from buried impact basins) and graben
    record early extension then stillness. The comparative lesson sharpens the
    Earth-based theory: subduction is the hard step of plate tectonics — the
    buoyancy problem of driving dense-enough lithosphere into the mantle — and
    Earth's water-softened, appropriately-dense plates manage it where the dry,
    buoyant lids of Venus and Mars do not. Every "no boundaries here" world is
    an experiment on why subduction works at all.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> without subduction, other worlds rift, wrinkle and stiffen instead
  of recycling their crust, and every signature is a statement about why subduction is the hard
  step. Two instruments finish the comparison: hot spots, which record whether the plate above
  moves, and craters, which time-stamp every surface.</p>

  <h2>Hot spots, crater clocks and the archive worlds</h2>
  <p>
    Two last instruments complete the comparison. <strong>Hot spots (2.8)</strong>
    build where plates don't move: Olympus Mons and the Tharsis shields on Mars,
    Venus's coronae, and on Earth the Hawaiian chain — the contrast is the
    argument: a fixed plume under a moving plate draws a trail; a fixed plume
    under a fixed plate draws a mountain. <strong>Craters</strong> are the
    planetary clock: surfaces accrue impact scars with (roughly) known flux, so
    counting craters dates terrains without a single sample — heavily cratered
    means ancient (the lunar highlands, ~4+ billion years), smooth means young
    (Venus's plains, the icy moons' resurfaced terrains). The lunar impact record
    dates the "Late Heavy Bombardment" around 3.9 billion years ago — though
    whether that was a true spike or an artefact of sampling is now debated
    (the expert keeps both readings open). And craters are warnings: Chelyabinsk
    (2013) — an unseen ~20-metre rock that airburst with the force of hundreds of
    kilotons, injuring about 1,500 through shattered glass — keeps the
    planetary-defence budget honest; the 1908 Tunguska event and the
    66-million-year-old Chicxulub impact (the dinosaur killer, a ~10 km bolide)
    bracket the stakes. The Moon, airless and unmoving, preserves all of it —
    Earth's archive of what has hit, and will hit, both worlds.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Venus is 'like early Earth', so studying it
    predicts our future." Venus's fate is not Earth's forecast: its runaway
    greenhouse was set by starting conditions (closer to the Sun, likely a
    different early water inventory and possibly no plate tectonics to run the
    carbon cycle) and it is not on Earth's trajectory this century or this
    millennium. The honest comparative lesson is structural, not predictive:
    climates have multiple stable states and sharp transitions between them —
    Venus shows that such a transition <em>exists</em>, the environmental-science
    pages treat whether one is being approached. Confusing the two readings is
    the standard error in both directions.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Olympus Mons stands about 22 km tall; Earth's hot-spot volcanoes build chains instead of single mountains. What difference between the two planets explains both shapes?</li>
    <li>InSight found Mars's core to be liquid, yet Mars has no magnetic field today. Reconcile the two facts.</li>
    <li>Why do craters let us date a surface without collecting a single rock — and why does the method need care on Venus?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Mars is a one-plate planet: the plume stayed fixed under one
    spot and stacked lava in place for billions of years, while Earth's moving plates carry each
    plume's output away as a trail — Hawaii, island by island. (2) A dynamo needs a
    <em>convecting</em> liquid-iron outer core, not merely a liquid one; Mars's dynamo died about
    4 billion years ago — the ancient southern crust stays magnetised, the younger north does not
    — so a liquid core is necessary but not sufficient. (3) Surfaces accrue impact scars at a
    roughly known flux, so counting craters dates the terrain; on Venus, the dense atmosphere
    destroys small incoming objects, so only craters about 30 km across and larger give usable
    counts — and those suggest young plains, evidence for recent global resurfacing.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comparative planetology runs Earth's geology checklist — drift, layers, volcanism, boundaries, hot spots, craters — on every body we can reach.</li>
    <li>Plate tectonics is a regime, not a default: it needs water-softened, breakable lithosphere and the right heat flow, which Venus's stagnant lid and Mars's one-plate crust lack.</li>
    <li>Differentiation is universal; a dynamo lives in the convecting liquid-iron outer core and can die (Mars, about 4 billion years ago) or never start (Venus today).</li>
    <li>Volcanism follows the heat source: radioactive youth on the Moon, tidal kneading on Io, fixed plumes on Mars, even water-ammonia lavas on the icy moons.</li>
    <li>Subduction is the hard step of plate tectonics, so every "no boundaries here" world is an experiment on why Earth manages it.</li>
    <li>Craters are clocks — more craters, older surface — and, from Chelyabinsk to Chicxulub, warnings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lesson <em>Geophysics of Plate Driving Forces</em> attacks the question
    this page kept open: why Earth's plates move at all, and what drives them. The advanced
    lessons <em>Paleoclimate and Ice Ages</em> and <em>Geochemical Cycles</em> then take the
    archive-world lesson — climates with multiple stable states, atmospheres that are
    geologically labile — back to Earth's own history.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The mission programme as a laboratory.</strong> Planetary geology
    advances by missions, and the current queue is unusually decisive: Europa
    Clipper (launched October 2024) will fly Europa dozens of times, mapping ice
    thickness and ocean depth with ice-penetrating radar; ESA's JUICE follows to
    Jupiter system, orbiting Ganymede; Dragonfly (launch toward 2028) will fly a
    nuclear-powered rotorcraft through Titan's dense atmosphere, hopping dune
    fields and sampling organic chemistry in situ; Perseverance has cached
    sample tubes in Jezero crater for a future return mission; and VERITAS and
    DAVINCI (Venus orbiters and probes, in NASA's queue) would finally give
    Venus the interior-and-atmosphere survey it has lacked since Magellan
    (1990–94). Each instrument choice is a hypothesis test: radar thicknesses
    test ocean models, plume fly-throughs test habitability chemistry, rotorcraft
    sediments test prebiotic synthesis. The geology runs on the mission
    calendar.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Exoplanet geology: the discipline's new frontier.</strong> The methods
    on this page are being ported to worlds we will never visit: transit
    spectroscopy reads atmospheres (and so, in principle, volcanic outgassing);
    Lava worlds (55 Cancri e and cousins, dayside molten rock) let astronomers
    measure a planet's composition from its thermal emission; JWST's
    atmosphere surveys test whether rocky planets around M-dwarfs retain air at
    all (the "cosmic shoreline" — a suspected energy-flux threshold above which
    atmospheres are stripped); and future large telescopes aim at direct imaging
    of Earth analogues. The plate-tectonics question has become
    exoplanetary: whether rocky exoplanets run carbon cycles — whether they
    are geologically alive — is now a proposed input to habitability
    assessments, decades before anyone could measure a ridge on a world 40
    light-years away. Planetary geology's comparative method is, in effect,
    being extended to a sample size of thousands.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What Earth's comparison has settled, and what it hasn't.</strong>
    Settled: differentiation is universal; impact cratering is a first-order
    surface process everywhere; atmospheres are geologically labile (Mars lost
    one, Venus gained a monstrous one); ice and methane run water-analogue
    cycles on cold worlds. Open: why Earth alone (so far) runs plate tectonics —
    water, size, timing, or luck; whether Venus resurfaced catastrophically or
    gradually (the crater distribution argues for uniformity, the resurfacing
    model for a pulse); whether the icy-moon oceans host hydrothermal
    chemistry like Earth's vents (Enceladus's hydrogen hints yes); and what
    the lunar impact record really says about the early Solar System's
    bombardment history. The comparative method's discipline is to hold all
    of these open while squeezing each new mission for one more line of
    evidence — the same posture as the driving-forces question, one planet
    over.
  </p>
